<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intaphrenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 520 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Intaphernes, Vindafarnâ, Vidafarnâ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Intaphrenes was a Persian, one of the seven who in September 522 BCE helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> seize the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>. He then became Darius's bow carrier, a high position in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> shows him. In 521 BCE Darius sent him at the head of an army against Arakha, who had claimed the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> IV; Babylon was taken without much fighting on 27 November, and Arakha and his chief followers were impaled. In the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Intaphrenes was later put to death after he cut off the ears and noses of two officers who barred his way to the king, and Darius, fearing a revolt, killed his family except his wife's brother and son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intaphernes.jpg" alt="Intaphrenes" width="330" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leen van Dorp · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Intaphrenes_on_the_Behistun_Relief.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Six of them descended from the nobles who joined Darius in 522 BCE to kill the man he called the usurper Gaumata: Otanes, <b>Intaphernes</b>, Gobryas, Hydarnes, Megabyzus and Aspathines.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
